<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Ambarri</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gallic people of the lower Saône and Ain, clients of the Aedui whose appeal for help helped draw Caesar into Gaul in 58 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Their name means those on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saone">Arar</a>, the ancient Saône. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> lists the Ambarri among the peoples who followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellovesus">Bellovesus</a> into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the legendary migrations of around 600 BCE. In Caesar's day they lived east of the Saône, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> confluence.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Caesar calls them friends and kinsmen of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a>, on whom they depended. No rulers or towns are recorded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the migrating <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetii">Helvetii</a> plundered their fields in 58 BCE, the Ambarri complained to Caesar, adding to his reasons to attack. After the conquest their territory was divided among neighbouring civitates, and the name survives only in a few place names in the Ain.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ambarri.jpg" alt="Ambarri" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Autorité émettrice de monnaie prétendue · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Stat%C3%A8re,_or,_fonds_celtique,_Imitation_du_stat%C3%A8re_de_Philippe_II_-_btv1b112873209_(2_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=ambarri" aria-label="Ambarri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4741 529 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ambarri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5095 786h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5061 785h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5061 785h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day France. <a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=ambarri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

